[FINAL-TEXT] **ROOT CAUSE FOUND.**

Router6 has an `iptables` **FORWARD chain ACL** with a catch-all DROP rule on its **eth2** interface (the router3-facing uplink):

```
iifname "eth2" ip saddr 172.16.10.0/24 ip daddr 172.18.51.1 tcp dport 80 accept
iifname "eth2" meta l4proto tcp drop
iifname "eth2" meta l4proto udp drop
iifname "eth2" counter packets 20 bytes 1680 drop   ← 20 packets actively dropped
```

**The asymmetry explained:**
- **Forward path** (PC15→PC11): PC15 → router6.**eth1** (in) → eth2 (out) → router3 → router4 → PC11. The ACL matches `iifname "eth2"` = ingress eth2, so the FORWARD rule does NOT fire on the outbound direction.
- **Return path** (PC11→PC15): PC11 → router4 → router3 → router6.**eth2** (in) → eth1 (out) → mswitch2 → PC15. Now `iifname "eth2"` matches — all protocols are dropped.
- **Router6's own pings** to 192.168.200.11 work because the ICMP replies arrive destined **to router6's own IP** (10.50.1.2) — they hit the INPUT chain, not FORWARD, so the ACL is bypassed entirely.

The 20 packets / 1680 bytes already dropped in the catch-all rule is live evidence of active blocking.

